683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/06/travel/06qna-giada-1/06qna-giada-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/06/travel/06qna-giada-1/06qna-giada-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/05/06/travel/06qna-giada-1/06qna-giada-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/06/travel/06qna-giada-1/06qna-giada-1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Where Giada De Laurentiis Loves to Go in Italy', 'kicker': 'Q & A', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Giada De Laurentiis On Italian Treasures.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Italy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'De Laurentiis, Giada', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-05-01T09:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': "By Kathryn O'Shea-Evans", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "O'Shea-Evans", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/45db6660-2b50-5224-bc07-64fe3bb83804', 'word_count': 750, 'uri': 'nyt://article/45db6660-2b50-5224-bc07-64fe3bb83804'}, {'abstract': 'A selection of books published this week; plus, a peek at what our colleagues around the newsroom are reading.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/05/01/books/review/new-noteworthy-stephanie-saul.html', 'snippet': 'A selection of books published this week; plus, a peek at what our colleagues around the newsroom are reading.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ORWELL ON TRUTH By George Orwell with an introduction by Adam Hochschild. (Houghton Mifflin Harcourt, $20.) Orwell has become a touchstone for those who see the pursuit of truth as imperiled. It was only a matter of time before his published thoughts about lying and its corrosive effects on politics and society would be gathered together for our worried, contemporary mind. THE GREAT NEW YORK SUBWAY MAP By Emiliano Ponzi. (Museum of Modern Art, $19.95.) We forget that such an everyday object like the New York City subway map was once actually a feat of graphic design. This illustrated book, intended for children but informative for all, reminds us of Massimo Vignelli’s great innovation. THE WORLD-ENDING FIRE By Wendell Berry. (Counterpoint, $26.) Berry, the defender of rural American lands and communities, holds on fiercely to a dying way of life in this collection of essays. He rails against the mass-produced and tries to rally our appreciation for the natural world. TRIP By Tao Lin. (Vintage, paper, $16.) Lin, the provocateur, produces his first book-length work of nonfiction, an exploration of psychedelic drugs and the realizations they opened up for him. ROOTED COSMOPOLITANS By James Loeffler. (Yale, $32.50.) This year marks two 70th anniversaries: Israel’s birth and the signing of the Universal Declaration of Human Rights. Loeffler reminds us how entwined the two events were — both for the role Jews played in codifying human rights and for how Zionism was buoyed by these new international values.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/06/books/review/06NewNoteworthy0/06NewNoteworthy0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 259, 'width': 549, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/06/books/review/06NewNoteworthy0/06NewNoteworthy0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 259, 'width': 549, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/06/books/review/06NewNoteworthy0/06NewNoteworthy0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/05/06/books/review/06NewNoteworthy0/06NewNoteworthy0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/06/books/review/06NewNoteworthy0/06NewNoteworthy0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'New & Noteworthy', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New &amp; Noteworthy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-05-01T09:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8a439b36-0459-52d2-9cbd-5a6d6e830f30', 'word_count': 452, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8a439b36-0459-52d2-9cbd-5a6d6e830f30'}, {'abstract': 'A Madeira-laced cream sauce with the fresh, crisp softness of chicken tastes like magic.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/05/01/magazine/spring-smothered-chicken-dinner.html', 'snippet': 'A Madeira-laced cream sauce with the fresh, crisp softness of chicken tastes like magic.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Chefs are not generally the best source for recipes. They don’t use them the way we do at home. Often they don’t use recipes at all. Many of them cook instinctively, the way artists work. They do not measure ingredients before cooking. They navigate by taste and smell. They respond to the sound of fat sizzling against a piece of fish, or the sight of a shallot turning golden in the heat. When they’re happy with the food, they jot down a few notes, then cook the dish again and again until it can be made by rote. The people who work for them watch and listen and smell and taste until they can do the same — professional cooking is about apprenticeship and technique. Most line cooks don’t receive formal recipes from which to cook. They get battlefield instruction for soldiers new to the front, sauce-stained cards scribbled with notes: sweat in rondeau, drop; roast, save fond; arroser fish; crash jus with stock. Civilians don’t cook that way.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/06/magazine/06mag-eat/06mag-06eat-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/05/06/magazine/06mag-eat/06mag-06eat-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/06/magazine/06mag-eat/06mag-06eat-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/06/magazine/06mag-eat/06mag-06eat-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 