'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Collins', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Bret', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stephens', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/84147e52-a116-55fe-9198-b634ee19535c', 'word_count': 2053, 'uri': 'nyt://article/84147e52-a116-55fe-9198-b634ee19535c'}, {'abstract': 'Consolidation would end the “wireless wars” that have lowered prices and improved service for consumers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/05/01/opinion/sprint-t-mobile-merger.html', 'snippet': 'Consolidation would end the “wireless wars” that have lowered prices and improved service for consumers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The merits of some mergers make for a close case, but the proposed merger between the mobile carriers Sprint and T-Mobile, which would create a new telecommunications behemoth, is not one of them. Basic economics strongly suggests the proposed combination should be dead on arrival, at least if the nation’s antitrust law still stands for competition and lower prices for consumers. In addition, the recent history of telecommunications and similar industries indicates that allowing consolidation to just three “majors” — Verizon, AT&T and the new T-Mobile (merged with Sprint) — is a terrible idea.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/01/opinion/01wuWeb/merlin_137493030_b3b4b81c-99b7-48ea-a2f9-05e72bce1072-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 392, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/05/01/opinion/01wuWeb/merlin_137493030_b3b4b81c-99b7-48ea-a2f9-05e72bce1072-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 392}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image'