7+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.J. Goldmann', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Goldmann', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/fff954bd-1849-5b14-bde1-cde886954581', 'word_count': 1073, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fff954bd-1849-5b14-bde1-cde886954581'}, {'abstract': 'Picking a candidate is not corrupt and undemocratic. That’s what party leadership is all about.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/05/03/opinion/dccc-democratic-primaries-interference.html', 'snippet': 'Picking a candidate is not corrupt and undemocratic. That’s what party leadership is all about.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee has come under fire for interfering in a handful of Democratic primary races around the country. In trying to clear the primary field for the more moderate candidate they think will be the strongest in the general election, party leaders have drawn the anger of some progressive Democrats who think that the mere attempt to do so is corrupt and undemocratic.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/03/opinion/03kamarck/merlin_135112002_04e7f0a0-e753-4748-affd-9598c6ee193f-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/05/03/opinion/03kamarck/merlin_135112002_04e7f0a0-e753-4748-affd-9598c6ee193f-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/03/opinion/03kamarck/merlin_135112002_04e7f0a0-e753-4748-affd-9598c6ee193f-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 597, 'width': 10