alue': 'New York Times', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reading and Writing Skills (Education)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-05-03T18:49:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Learning', 'section_name': 'The Learning Network', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Learning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c913e328-a88f-5eb9-8eec-0840a9e9dd91', 'word_count': 9817, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c913e328-a88f-5eb9-8eec-0840a9e9dd91'}, {'abstract': 'He could use his power over a new mass medium to unite the country. Instead, he uses it to divide us.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/05/03/opinion/trump-father-coughlin-roosevelt.html', 'snippet': 'He could use his power over a new mass medium to unite the country. Instead, he uses it to divide us.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Everything seemed to be falling apart. After his election to the presidency in November 1932, Franklin D. Roosevelt received a talkative friend. If Roosevelt could rescue America from the Great Depression, the friend said, then he would be remembered as the greatest of presidents; if he failed, he would go down as the worst. There were alternatives to democratic capitalism afoot in the world: fascism in Germany and Italy, communism in Russia. Listening to his visitor, Roosevelt was matter-of-fact. “If I fail,” he said, “I shall be the last one.”', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/06/sunday-review/06Meacham/06Meacham-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 447, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/05/06/sunday-review/06M