mery Advertiser (Newspaper)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ALABAMA', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ARKANSAS', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Montgomery (Ala)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Southern States (US)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-05-05T18:30:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brent Staples', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brent', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Staples', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/2a22cd2e-c088-5fbc-8425-30a504400eb6', 'word_count': 826, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2a22cd2e-c088-5fbc-8425-30a504400eb6'}, {'abstract': 'As I attend Eurovision events across the Continent, I see that a different Europe is possible.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/05/05/opinion/sunday/eurovision-europe.html', 'snippet': 'As I attend Eurovision events across the Continent, I see that a different Europe is possible.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LISBON — On April 7, the Russian singer Yuliya Samoylova sat in her wheelchair on a makeshift stage inside Vegas Kuntsevo, a Moscow shopping mall “created in the Italian style.” Behind her, a facade of Rome’s Colosseum. Before her, a replica of Pisa’s Leaning Tower. And between those two landmarks, a thousand screaming fans listening to her sing “I Won’t Break,” her entry for this year’s Eurovision Song Contest here in Lisbon.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/06/sunday-review/06adams/06adams-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarg