8-05-05T18:30:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Lee Adams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': 'Lee', 'lastname': 'Adams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/495dbda3-457e-5a43-994d-660957da003a', 'word_count': 1239, 'uri': 'nyt://article/495dbda3-457e-5a43-994d-660957da003a'}, {'abstract': 'Our government could learn a thing or two about efficiency and cooperation from America’s wetlands.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/05/05/opinion/sunday/stop-calling-washington-a-swamp-its-offensive-to-swamps.html', 'snippet': 'Our government could learn a thing or two about efficiency and cooperation from America’s wetlands.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Houston — Washington is not a swamp and never was. Would that it were. (The tale that the Capitol was built on a drained swamp is apocryphal, I’m told.) The political expression “drain the swamp” has been traced back to Socialists in the early 1900s, during a time when swamps were drained to reduce the populations of malaria-carrying mosquitoes. For over a century, politicians have used the phrase to go after the perceived bloodsuckers of their day — lobbyists, corrupt officials, wasteful spenders. But after having killed half the wetlands in our country, we should not want to drain any more swamps.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/06/opinion/sunday/06serpas/06serpas-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 384, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/05/06/opinion/sunday/06serpas/06serpas-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 384}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/06/opinion/sunday/06serpas/06serpas-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 655, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/06/opinion/sunday/06serpas/06serpas-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1311, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/06/opinion/sunday/06serpas/06serpas-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/05/06/opinion/sunday/06serpas/06serpas-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/06/opinion/sunday/06serpas/06serpas-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Stop Calling Washington a Swamp. It’s Offensive to Swamps.', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Don’t Drain the Swamp', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wetlands', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sustainable Living', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-05-05T18:30:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Martha Serpas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Martha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Serpas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/7613907a-9b5b-5306-b3c0-f19005cd0a97', 'word_count': 812, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7613907a-9b5b-5306-b3c0-f19005cd0a97'}, {'abstract': 'Why the turn to sex scandal and his party’s lack of an agenda might actually be helping the president.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/05/05/opinion/sunday/how-trump-is-winning.html', 'snippet': 'Why the turn to sex scandal and his party’s lack of an agenda might actually be helping the president.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As Donald Trump entered the second year in office, he seemed determined to destabilize his administration’s fragile status quo. He pushed out a cluster of advisers, replacing them with people he liked watching on TV. He forged ahead on his long-promised policy of trade war. He decided to take the starring, summit-organizing role in his own North Korean brinksmanship. And he stepped up — well, or just continued, it’s hard to make quantitative judgments — his rhetorical war against the Robert Mueller investigation.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/06/opinion/sunday/06Douthat/06Douthat-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/05/06/opinion/sunday/06Douthat/06Douthat-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/06/opinion/sunday/06Douthat/06Douthat-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/06/opinion/sunday/06Douthat/06Douthat-superJumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/06/opinion/sunday/06Douthat/06Douthat-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/05/06/opinion/sunday/06Douthat/06Douthat-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/06/opinion/sunday/06Douthat/06Douthat-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How Trump Is Winning', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Columnist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How the President Is Winning', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North Korea', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-05-05T18:30:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/cf6c87be-d5be-584f-9a62-f1a690ed9866', 'word_count': 799, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cf6c87be-d5be-584f-9a62-f1a690ed9866'}, {'abstract': 'We’re ignoring more important issues as we obsess on the nonstop scandals and outrages.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/05/05/opinion/sunday/trump-obsession.html', 'snippet': 'We’re ignoring more important issues as we obsess on the nonstop scandals and outrages.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We in the commentariat complain about President Trump, but we’re locked in a symbiotic relationship with him.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/06/sunday-review/06kristof/06kristof-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/05/06/sunday-review/06kristof/06kristof-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/06/sunday-review/06kristof/06kristof-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/06/sunday-review/06kristof/06kristof-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/06/sunday-review/06kristof/06kristof-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/05/06/sunday-review/06kristof/06kristof-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/06/sunday-review/06kristof/06kristof-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Our Addiction to Trump', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Columnist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Our Addiction  To Trump', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-05-05T18:30:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas Kristof', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kristof', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/3206dd4b-12a6-5d09-b141-8c2b56ad3e74', 'word_count': 925, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3206dd4b-12a6-5d09-b141-8c2b56ad3e74'}, {'abstract': 'Thanks to artists like Donald Glover and Janelle Monáe, we’re seeing a weirder, cooler, queerer and more realistic black and sexy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/05/05/opinion/sunday/the-new-black-hotties.html', 'snippet': 'Thanks to artists like Donald Glover and Janelle Monáe, we’re seeing a weirder, cooler, queerer and more realistic black and sexy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There is a new black and sexy. Notions of attractiveness, from the type of partner we want to the aesthetic choices we make for ourselves, have shifted from glistening abs and perfect facial symmetry toward something more realistic, inclusive and, in many ways, cooler. Artists like Donald Glover, Issa Rae and Janelle Monáe have allowed us to see a more complex world reflected back to us, validating black nerds, black queers and black weirdos everywhere. Black and sexy has blossomed into a celebration of eccentricity.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/06/opinion/sunday/06bowen/06bowen-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 698,