ts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeremy Egner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeremy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Egner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2d7d692a-4dfa-50b2-a7c0-9c915a9f7ffb', 'word_count': 290, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2d7d692a-4dfa-50b2-a7c0-9c915a9f7ffb'}, {'abstract': 'Men monopolized the spotlight of the May ’68 uprising in France, but women played a role, too. And the protests changed their lives.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/05/07/opinion/france-protests-68-women.html', 'snippet': 'Men monopolized the spotlight of the May ’68 uprising in France, but women played a role, too. And the protests changed their lives.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — One of my biggest regrets is that I was born too late for the barricades of May 1968. I was only 4 years old, and so I missed this huge “happening,” the student and worker uprising that brought France to a standstill. My mother’s sister, who was then a 22-year-old elementary school teacher here, recalled with nostalgia the strange atmosphere in the city, where, she said, “the daily routine stopped and everyone went on a picnic.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/07/opinion/07maier/07maier-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 402, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/05/07/opinion/07maier/07maier-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 402}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/07/opinion/07maier/07maier-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 686, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/07/opinion/07maier/07maier-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1372, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/07/opinion/07maier/07maier-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/05/07/opinion/07maier/07maier-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/07/opinion/07maier/07maier-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Hidden Women of Paris', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': "Women's Rights", 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests and Riots', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pisier, Marie-France', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wiazemsky, Anne', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-05-07T15:24:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Corinne Maier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Corinne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Maier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/32dd09bb-ca80-54d5-9ab6-0e498f16ad51', 'word_count': 924, 'uri': 'nyt://article/32dd09bb-ca80-54d5-9ab6-0e498f16ad51'}, {'abstract': 'A look at one of the entries from last week’s puzzles that stumped our solvers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/05/07/crosswords/what-the-heck-is-that-oed.html', 'snippet': 'A look at one of the entries from last week’s puzzles that stumped our solvers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SPECIAL POST — The Oxford English Dictionary, or OED as it is commonly known, is one of the best known references on the English language, yet a devious clue — “It ends with ‘zyzzyva,’ in brief” — stumped many of our solvers in the Thursday, May 3 crossword puzzle by Emily Carroll. It has appeared in The New York Times Crossword a total of 139 times.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/07/crosswords/07wordplay-heck-oed2/merlin_118477805_167d0b1e-b18f-4f11-ae67-c9c5b81120e7-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/05/07/crosswords/07wordplay-heck-oed2/merlin_118477805_167d0b1e-b18f-4f11-ae67-c9c5b81120e7-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/07/crosswords/07wordplay-heck-oed2/merlin_118477805_167d0b1e-b18f-4f11-ae67-c9c5b81120e7-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/07/crosswords/07wordplay-heck-oed2/merlin_118477805_167d0b1e-b18f-4f11-ae67-c9c5b81120e7-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/07/crosswords/07wordplay-heck-oed2/merlin_118477805_167d0b1e-b18f-4f11-ae67-c9c5b81120e7-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_