mes', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/20/travel/20PTG-Chile-PROMO/20PTG-Chile-PROMO-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/05/20/travel/20PTG-Chile-PROMO/20PTG-Chile-PROMO-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/20/travel/20PTG-Chile-PROMO/20PTG-Chile-PROMO-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/20/travel/20PTG-Chile-PROMO/20PTG-Chile-PROMO-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/20/travel/20PTG-Chile-PROMO/20PTG-Chile-PROMO-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/05/20/travel/20PTG-Chile-PROMO/20PTG-Chile-PROMO-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/20/travel/20PTG-Chile-PROMO/20PTG-Chile-PROMO-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The 52 Places Traveler: In Chile, a Gorgeous, Very Rainy and Sometimes Lonely Journey', 'kicker': 'Place 11', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Splendor and Solitude on Chile’s Route of Parks', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chile', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Patagonia (Chile)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Parks, Monuments and Seashores', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hikes and Hiking', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Volcanoes', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-05-08T09:00:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jada Yuan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jada', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Yuan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2c70ed6e-a5dd-5888-9d16-383f179ce2bb', 'word_count': 2206, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2c70ed6e-a5dd-5888-9d16-383f179ce2bb'}, {'abstract': 'It’s supposed to be a compliment, but it saddles the work — and its audience — with the weight of moral imperative.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/05/08/magazine/what-do-we-mean-when-we-call-art-necessary.html', 'snippet': 'It’s supposed to be a compliment, but it saddles the work — and its audience — with the weight of moral imperative.', 'lead_paragraph': 'About a year ago I met up for the first time with a woman I knew only online. Articulate and funny, she is a brilliant writer who studied literature in graduate school. So I was surprised that, when I mentioned a recent novel I liked, my new friend responded with head-shaking resignation. “I can’t see how anyone justifies talking about books anymore,” she said. Our nation was so overwhelmed with causes demanding attention and action, she suggested, that it had entered a state of constant emergency, whereby pursuits both personal and political must be pitted against one another to determine which are essential.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0