ide in democratic constitutions themselves.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/05/08/opinion/democracy-authoritarian-constitutions.html', 'snippet': 'The roots of backslides to authoritarianism reside in democratic constitutions themselves.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Italy, Poland, Hungary and even Spain: European democracy is in shambles. Critical threats to democracy have also surfaced in countries like Turkey, Brazil and the Philippines. Under President Trump’s “America First” orientation, leaders with authoritarian tendencies in places as disparate as Egypt, Honduras, Russia and Venezuela have trampled their political opponents without concern for anything more harmful than a tongue lashing from the United States.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/08/opinion/08albertus-menaldo/08albertus-menaldo-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/05/08/opinion/08albertus-menaldo/08albertus-menaldo-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/08/opinion/08albertus-menaldo/08albertus-menaldo-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/08/opinion/08albertus-menaldo/08albertus-menaldo-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1363, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/08/opinion/08albertus-menaldo/08albertus-menaldo-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/05/08/opinion/08albertus-menaldo/08albertus-menaldo-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/08/opinion/08albertus-menaldo/08albertus-menaldo-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Are So Many Democracies Breaking Down?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Constitutions', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Constitutional Amendments', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Constitution (US)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Democracy (Theory and Philosophy)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Legislatures and Parliaments', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Religion-State Relations', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fidesz Party', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Justice and Development Party (Turkey)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National League for Democracy (Myanmar)', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Aung San Suu Kyi', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Erdogan, Recep Tayyip', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hungary', 'rank': 15, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Turkey', 'rank': 16, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-05-08T14:30:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Albertus and Victor Menaldo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Albertus', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Victor', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Menaldo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/92fff0dd-79f0-51c4-ab88-7e3ace10ec99', 'word_count': 1128, 'uri': 'nyt://article/92fff0dd-79f0-51c4-ab88-7e3ace10ec99'}, {'abstract': 'A selection of books published this week; plus, a peek at what our colleagues around the newsroom are reading.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/05/08/books/review/new-noteworthy-jake-silverstein.html', 'snippet': 'A selection of books published this week; plus, a peek at what our colleagues around the newsroom are reading.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE ASHTRAY By Errol Morris. (University of Chicago, $30.) In 1972, Thomas Kuhn, the philosopher of science who invented the idea of “paradigm shifts,” threw an ashtray at Morris, then a graduate student. The two disagreed about the nature of truth and reality. This book is Morris’s attempt to throw that ashtray back. THE MATHEMATICS OF THE BREATH AND THE WAY By Charles Bukowski. (City Lights, paper, $16.95.) The old, drunken lech offers his ideas about writing and the art of trying to live as a writer in a series of collected interviews and essays. THE BOOK OF WHY By Judea Pearl and Dana Mackenzie. (Basic Books, $32.) Pearl, winner of the 2011 Turing Award (as well as the father of Daniel Pearl), has focused his research on causality. How can we know whether it was rain or a sprinkler that made a sidewalk wet or if a specific drug cured an illness? This book breaks down his scientific theory. LIGHTING THE FIRES OF FREEDOM By Janet Dewart Bell. (The New Press, $25.99.) African-American women were central to the civil rights movement, but seldom get their due. Bell looks to move beyond Rosa Parks to show the fuller panoply by profiling nine largely forgotten women who were also on the front lines. WHEN EINSTEIN WALKED WITH GÖDEL By Jim Holt. (Farrar, Straus & Giroux, $28.) Does time exist? What is infinity? What is quantum physics? Holt offers a series of previously published essays and sketches of scientific thinkers that answer these questions and other mysteries from the world of physics and math.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/13/books/review/13newnoteworthy0/13newnoteworthy2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 250, 'width': 553, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/13/books/review/13newnoteworthy0/13newnoteworthy2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 250, 'width': 553, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/13/books/review/13newnoteworthy0/13newnoteworthy2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/05/13/books/review/13newnoteworthy0/13newnoteworthy2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/13/books/review/13newnoteworthy0/13newnoteworthy2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'New & Noteworthy', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New &amp; Noteworthy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-05-08T14:38:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2a0f11f6-3d65-5798-b88c-3f5d94d09c6d', 'word_count': 432, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2a0f11f6-3d65-5798-b88c-3f5d94d09c6d'}, {'abstract': 'At least five more top managers are leaving the world’s largest sports footwear and apparel company after an investigation into complaints of harassment and bias. The departures follow six others.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/05/08/business/nike-harassment.html', 'snippet': 'At least five more top managers are leaving the world’s largest sports footwear and apparel company after an investigation into complaints of harassment and bias. The departures follow six others.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A sweeping investigation into workplace behavior at Nike has resulted in the departures of five more top-level executives, raising to 11 the number of senior managers to leave the company as it continues to overhaul its upper ranks amid widespread allegations of harassment and discrimination against female employees.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/08/business/nikedepartures-3/nikedepartures-3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/05/08/business/nikedepartures-3/nikedepartures-3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 450}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/08/business/nikedepartures-3/nikedepartures-3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo