ting the paradisiacal archipelago on the map.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a mirroring of Norse legends, a mid-1300s Medici atlas showed an archipelago floating in the middle of the Atlantic. More than half a century later, the Azores — nine volcanic isles about 800 miles west of Portugal — were more formally discovered, presumably by the Portuguese explorer Diogo de Silves. Some say that the islands’ name refers to the cerulean hues of the surrounding waters; others believe it has to do with the birds flying overhead (‘‘açor’’ can mean goshawk in Portuguese).', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '74', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/01/t-magazine/01tmag-azores-slide-V56T-copy/01tmag-azores-slide-V56T-copy-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/05/01/t-magazine/01tmag-azores-slide-V56T-copy/01tmag-azores-slide-V56T-copy-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/01/t-magazine/01tmag-azores-slide-V56T-copy/01tmag-azores-slide-V56T-copy-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/01/t-magazine/01tmag-azores-slide-V56T-copy/01tmag-azores-slide-V56T-copy-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 702, 'width': 1052, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/01/t-magazine/01tmag-azores-slide-V56T-copy/01tmag-azores-slide-V56T-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'im