0T09:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Climate', 'section_name': 'Climate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brad Plumer and Nadja Popovich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Plumer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Nadja', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Popovich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/89e0c325-85af-5d2e-be6f-65403fc3be34', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/89e0c325-85af-5d2e-be6f-65403fc3be34'}, {'abstract': 'Jazz, street murals, distinctive architecture and world-class breweries in Sweden’s second-largest city.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/05/10/travel/gothenburg-sweden-what-to-do.html', 'snippet': 'Jazz, street murals, distinctive architecture and world-class breweries in Sweden’s second-largest city.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Swedes didn’t always adore Gothenburg. The resurgent second city, equidistant between Copenhagen and Oslo on Sweden’s western coast, was long viewed as a grungy port in slow decline. But locals saw the potential. Languishing neighborhoods became incubators for improvisational creative projects — in music, art, design, dining — and sparked a renaissance that spread from one district to another. Today, the city’s blue-and-white trams still rumble down the wide boulevards, but visitors will also find a diverse music scene, inspiring art, world-class breweries, trendsetting shops and gregarious locals determined to convince you that this west coast is the best coast.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/27/travel/27SubInstagram/merlin_137692203_19516df2-c43e-4543-8b6d-b03702178cb9-articleLarge.j