, Donald J', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Europe', 'rank': 15, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 16, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-05-10T14:30:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Veronique de Rugy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Veronique', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'de Rugy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/a5dc8f50-1a76-5149-b63a-2d2dbcaa3c4a', 'word_count': 1028, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a5dc8f50-1a76-5149-b63a-2d2dbcaa3c4a'}, {'abstract': 'Once it was widely planted in Germany and Alsace and easy to find, but bad practices made for bad wine, which ruined the market. Can it come back?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/05/10/dining/drinks/sylvaner-wine.html', 'snippet': 'Once it was widely planted in Germany and Alsace and easy to find, but bad practices made for bad wine, which ruined the market. Can it come back?', 'lead_paragraph': 'With the overwhelming number of wines available today from all over the world, in a vast diversity of styles, many made from grapes virtually unknown a generation ago, consumers have had to resort to shortcuts and workarounds to slice through the confusion.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/16/dining/16Pour1/16Pour1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/05/16/dining/16Pour1/16Pour1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/16/dining/16Pour1/16Pour1-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height'