23:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Chappatte', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chappatte', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/6287dcfe-9bd4-54ad-9c4d-70714ab6263f', 'word_count': 30, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6287dcfe-9bd4-54ad-9c4d-70714ab6263f'}, {'abstract': 'Soul-stirring Spanish American art is finally being awarded center stage status in North American museums — and it’s sensuous and rapturous.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/05/10/arts/design/review-painted-in-mexico-metropolitan-museum-of-art-.html', 'snippet': 'Soul-stirring Spanish American art is finally being awarded center stage status in North American museums — and it’s sensuous and rapturous.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The great cathedral of Mexico City is a visual opera of light, movement and sacred history. And at its center is a true coup de théâtre: the carved and painted “Retablo de los Reyes” — “Altar of the Kings” — by the 18th-century artist Juan Rodríguez Juárez. At once soaring and torrential, the altar insists on your smallness, your next-to-nothingness in the redemptive drama. At the same time, by overwhelming and engulfing you it makes you part of the story. You’re both audience and participant, a vital particle.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/11/arts/11paintedinmexico10/11paintedinmexico10-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 837, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/05/11/arts/11paintedinmexico10/11paintedinmexico10-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 837}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/11/arts/11paintedinmexico10/11paintedinmexico10-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 735, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/11/arts/11paintedinmexico10/11paintedinmexico10-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1438, 'width': 1031, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/11/arts/11paintedinmexico10/11paintedinmexico10-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/05/11/arts/11paintedinmexico10/11paintedinmexico10-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/11/arts/11paintedinmexico10/11paintedinmexico10-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Painted in Mexico’: When a New Art Flourished Far From Mother Spain', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Treasures of the New World', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Museum of Art', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mexico', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Painted in Mexico, 1700-1790: Pinxit Mexici (Book)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-05-10T19:23:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Week