 planet, researchers say the rise of global trade helped spread the disease that has wiped out many amphibians.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the 1970s and 1980s frogs and other amphibians seemed to be disappearing overnight. By 1999, researchers had determined the culprit was a deadly disease caused by chytrid fungus which infected the animals with tiny, swimming spores. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/15/science/11TB-FUNGUS1/11TB-FUNGUS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/05/15/science/11TB-FUNGUS1/11TB-FUNGUS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/15/science/11TB-FUNGUS1/11TB-FUNGUS1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/15/science/11TB-FUNGUS1/11TB-FUNGUS1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/15/science/11TB-FUNGUS1/11TB-FUNGUS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/05/15/science/11TB-FUNGUS1/11TB-FUNGUS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/15/science/11TB-FUNGUS1/11TB-FUNGUS1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Frog-Killing Fungus Found to Have Origins on Korean Peninsula', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Strain: Frog-Killing Fungus Has Korean Origins', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AMPHIBIANS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Frogs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fungi', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Korea', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Genetics and Heredity', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North Korea', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Far East, South and Southeast Asia and Pacific Areas', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Science (Journal)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fisher, Matthew C', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lips, Karen', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': "O'Hanlon, Simon", 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-05-10T22:29:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By JoAnna Klein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joanna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Klein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3d178908-a869-5ebd-8c16-7d3fd953db14', 'word_count': 653, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3d178908-a869-5ebd-8c16-7d3fd953db14'}, {'abstract': 'What the former New York attorney general is accused of isn’t “choking.” It’s a specific act of domination and control over women.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/05/10/opinion/schneiderman-strangulation-control-women.html', 'snippet': 'What the former New York attorney general is accused of isn’t “choking.” It’s a specific act of domination and control over women.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It doesn’t take much to cut off someone’s oxygen supply, or to restrict blood flow to and from her brain. Around 10 seconds of pressure on the carotid artery, either constant or intermittent, is usually enough to render the victim unconscious, and it requires less pressure than it takes to open a can of soda.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/10/opinion/10Manne-Sub2/10Manne-Sub2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/05/10/opinion/10Manne-Sub2/10Manne-Sub2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/10/opinion/10Manne-Sub2/10Manne-Sub2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/10/opinion/10Manne-Sub2/10Manne-Sub2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/10/opinion/10Manne-Sub2/10Manne-Sub2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/05/10/opinion/10Manne-Sub2/10Manne-Sub2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/10/opinion/10Manne-Sub2/10Manne-Sub2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Eric Schneiderman and the Meaning of Strangulation', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Domestic Violence', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schneiderman, Eric T', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-05-10T22:44:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kate Manne', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Manne', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/0fbe1938-3911-52bf-a1be-71c301add51b', 'word_count': 967, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0fbe1938-3911-52bf-a1be-71c301add51b'}, {'abstract': 'A shady business history pays foreign policy dividends.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/05/10/opinion/donald-trumps-lizard-wisdom.html', 'snippet': 'A shady business history pays foreign policy dividends.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We’re all educated by our peers, and, over the years, a good portion of Donald Trump’s peers have been thugs. Operating in the New York construction world meant dealing with S&A Concrete, co-owned by “Fat Tony” Salerno of the Genovese crime family, and John Cody, the notorious head of Teamsters Local 282, who was convicted on racketeering and tax evasion charges.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/10/opinion/10brooksWeb/merlin_137934891_7e44b795-f2cd-4db9-a1ce-653eb85c22dc-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 391, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/05/10/opinion/10brooksWeb/merlin_137934891_7e44b795-f2cd-4db9-a1ce-6