ticle/f1992881-4051-51db-88ba-008abb50f7a7', 'word_count': 499, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f1992881-4051-51db-88ba-008abb50f7a7'}, {'abstract': 'Eating at immigrant-run restaurants is a way to signal “I’m glad you’re here” and “Please don’t give up.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/05/14/opinion/immigrant-restaurants-nashville.html', 'snippet': 'Eating at immigrant-run restaurants is a way to signal “I’m glad you’re here” and “Please don’t give up.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'NASHVILLE — Not quite two weeks ago, I was driving down Nolensville Road, Nashville’s “international corridor,” looking for a restaurant called Tennessee Halal Fried Chicken. In the passenger seat was John T. Edge, the director of the Southern Foodways Alliance and author of “The Potlikker Papers: A Food History of the Modern South.” He was telling me that this particular approach to dining out, in one way of looking at it, could be considered a form of exploitation: “To patronize a restaurant of people who are different from you can be a kind of booty call,” he said.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/14/opinion/14renklWeb/merlin_58349788_832d294e-bad8-4e32-94d6-96f61e9f8418-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/05/14/opinion/14renklWeb/merlin_58349788_832d294e-bad8-4e32-94d6-96f61e9f8418-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/14/opinion/14renklWeb/merlin_58349788_832d294e-bad8-4e32-94d6-96f61e9f8418-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/14/opinion/