s in the 1960s. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/05/15/lens/50-years-after-mug-shots-portraits-of-mississippi-freedom-riders.html', 'snippet': 'The journalist and photographer Eric Etheridge provides visual and oral histories of the courageous men and women known as the Freedom Riders in the 1960s. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'For seven months in 1961, hundreds of black and white volunteers descended on Southern bus and train stations. These Freedom Riders, as they were called, occupied segregated waiting areas, lunch counters, and restrooms in an attempt to compel the federal government to do what local authorities would not: enforce a U.S. Supreme Court ruling that declared discrimination in interstate public transportation illegal. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/16/lens/16freedomriders1/15freedomriders1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 415, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/05/16/lens/16freedomriders1/15freedomriders1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 415}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/16/lens/16freedomriders1/15freedomriders1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 708, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/16/lens/16freedomriders1/15freedomriders1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1417, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/16/lens/16freedomriders1/15freedomriders1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/05/16/lens/16freedomriders1/15freedomriders1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/16/lens/16freedomriders1/15freedomriders1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '50 Years After Their Mug Shots, Portraits of Mississippi’s Freedom Riders', 'kicker': 'Race Stories', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil Rights Movement (1954-68)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Etheridge, Eric', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests and Riots', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mug Shots', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-05-15T08:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Photo', 'section_name': 'Lens', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maurice Berger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maurice', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Berger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3a9b495d-c0ae-5e49-92df-b1c48134789a', 'word_count': 975, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3a9b495d-c0ae-5e49-92df-b1c48134789a'}, {'abstract': 'The Lyft president on self-driving cars, climate change and not reinventing the bus.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/05/15/magazine/john-zimmer-would-rather-die-than-take-an-uber.html', 'snippet': 'The Lyft president on self-driving cars, climate change and not reinventing the bus.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When ride-sharing apps made their debut a few years ago, lots of people thought it was crazy to get in a stranger’s car, and now we all do it at 2 a.m. without even thinking. Did you expect attitudes to change so quickly? It definitely moved faster than, I think, anyone — including myself — thought. We knew that we would need to change people’s behavior. It actually was one of the main reasons the original tagline was “your friend with a car.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '70', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/20/magazine/20mag-Talk2/20mag-Talk2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/05/20/magazine/20mag-Talk2/20mag-Talk2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/20/magazine/20mag-Talk2/20mag-Talk2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/20/magazine/20mag-Talk2/20mag-Talk2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/20/magazine/20mag-Talk2/20mag-Talk2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/05/20/magazine/20mag-Talk2/20mag-Talk2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/20/magazine/20mag-Talk2/20mag-Talk2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'John Zimmer Would Rather Die Than Take an Uber', 'kicker': 'Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'John Zimmer Would Rather Die Than Take An Uber', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Car Services and Livery Cabs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Driverless and Semiautonomous Vehicles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lyft Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zimmer, John (1984- )', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-05-15T09:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dan Amira', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amira', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2d4129c6-9dcc-5a25-9707-ab962c4f2b91', 'word_count': 710, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2d4129c6-9dcc-5a25-9707-ab962c4f2b91'}, {'abstract': 'Aerobic fitness may help you avoid lapses in your vocabulary.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/05/15/magazine/exercise-language-muscle-memory-word-recall-aging-fitness.html', 'snippet': 'Aerobic fitness may help you avoid lapses in your vocabulary.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Call them tip-of-the-tongue moments: those times we can’t quite call up the name or word that we know we know. These frustrating lapses are thought to be caused by a brief disruption in the brain’s ability to access a word’s sounds. We haven’t forgotten the word, and we know its meaning, but its formulation dances teasingly just beyond our grasp. Though these mental glitches are common throughout life, they become more frequent with age. Whether this is an inevitable part of growing older or somehow lifestyle-dependent is unknown. But because evidence already shows that physically fit older people have reduced risks for a variety of cognitive deficits, researchers recently looked into the relationship between aerobic fitness and word recall. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/20/magazine/20Well-01/20Well-01-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/05/20/magazine/20Well-01/20Well-01-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/20/magazine/20Well-01/20Well-01-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/20/magazine/20Well-01/20Well-01-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/20/magazine/20Well-01/20Well-01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/05/20/magazine/20Well-01/20Well-01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/20/magazine/20Well-01/20Well-01-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How Exercise Can Help You Recall Words', 'kicker': 'Well ', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Muscle Memory', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Exercise', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Psychology and Psychologists', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Memory', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Age, Chronological', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Language and Languages', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elderly', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-05-15T09:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gretchen Reynolds', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gretchen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Reynolds', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3faf9e15-8422-5b78-9520-619ef336ad31', 'word_count': 490, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3faf9e15-8422-5b78-9520-619ef336ad31'}, {'abstract': 'The tennis star leans toward natural products and — no surprise — expects to see results. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/05/15/style/serena-williams-beauty-routine.html', 'snippet': 'The tennis star leans toward natural products and — no surprise — expects to see results. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'The tennis great Serena Williams recently had a baby (a girl named Olympia), took up running and is putting in hours of training time to get fully back on the pro tennis circuit. In short, she has a lot on her plate. But Ms. Williams, 36, who travels between Palm Beach Gardens, Fla., and San Francisco, also carves out time for her beauty regimen, which is a mix of natural-leaning products and ones chosen to produce results. See what she’s using, below.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'cr