one, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/43343d72-41eb-51ca-950e-d87db2bb60e2', 'word_count': 674, 'uri': 'nyt://article/43343d72-41eb-51ca-950e-d87db2bb60e2'}, {'abstract': 'On submerging oneself in the language, the place and the unsettling allure of ‘Watership Down.’', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/05/15/t-magazine/watership-down-hampshire-england.html', 'snippet': 'On submerging oneself in the language, the place and the unsettling allure of ‘Watership Down.’', 'lead_paragraph': 'You spend the majority of your day underground and hide your newborn young deep in the earth. When you do surface, you lick the pads of your forepaws, coating them with saliva, then smooth them along the fur of your face. Listen, in the tall grass. Your ears are tall, funnels, and all sound is of course a movement; when the movement stops, it is only a kind of memory. What was that sound? A distant hrududu, out on the roadway? Approaching elil? It is wise to be cautious, ready to thump your long hind paws, to sound a warning. If you stand on your haunches to see over the tall grass, everything that sees you — the Thousand, the elil — will try to kill you. The only reassurance, one you hold close: My people are the strongest in the world, for they breed faster and eat more than any of the other people.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '138', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/26/t-magazine/26tmag-watership-slide-Q6TV-copy/26tmag-watership-slide-Q6TV-copy-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/26/t-magazine/26tmag-watership-slide-Q6TV-copy/26tmag-watership-slide-Q6TV-copy-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/26/t-magazine/26tmag-watership-slide-Q6TV-copy/26tmag-watership-slide-Q6TV-copy-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/26/t-magazine/26tmag-watership-slide-Q6TV-copy/26tmag-watership-slide-Q6TV-copy-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1333, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/26/t-magazine/26tmag-watership-slide-Q6TV-copy/26tmag-watership-slide-Q6TV-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/26/t-magazine/26tmag-watership-slide-Q6TV-copy/26tmag-watership-slide-Q6TV-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/26/t-magazine/26tmag-watership-slide-Q6TV-copy/26tmag-watership-slide-Q6TV-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'On the Unsettling Allure of ‘Watership Down’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Crouched Within Golden Grass', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rabbits', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Adams, Richard (1920-2016)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hampshire (England)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-05-15T14:38:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Rock', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rock', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/01bf70da-3c77-5866-8fbe-5938b355e1b1', 'word_count': 3091, 'uri': 'nyt://article/01bf70da-3c77-5866-8fbe-5938b355e1b1'}, {'abstract': 'He wrote “The Electric Kool-Aid Acid Test,” “Bonfire of the Vanities” and “The Right Stuff,” and pioneered a novelistic form of journalism in the 1960s and ’70s.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/05/15/obituaries/tom-wolfe-pyrotechnic-nonfiction-writer-and-novelist-dies-at-88.html', 'snippet': 'He wrote “The Electric Kool-Aid Acid Test,” “Bonfire of the Vanities” and “The Right Stuff,” and pioneered a novelistic form of journalism in the 1960s and ’70s.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tom Wolfe, an innovative journalist and novelist whose technicolor, wildly punctuated prose brought to life the worlds of California surfers, car customizers, astronauts and Manhattan’s moneyed status-seekers in works like “The Kandy-Kolored Tangerine-Flake Streamline Baby,” “The Right Stuff” and “Bonfire of the Vanities,” died on Monday in a Manhattan hospital. He was 88.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/17/obituaries/16WOLFEsub-2/16WOLFEsub-2-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 819, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/05/17/obituaries/16WOLFEsub-2/16WOLFEsub-2-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 819}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/17/obituaries/16WOLFEsub-2/16WOLFEsub-2-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 750, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'super