{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Reyburn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/66ac2cf0-f9f6-5b73-80fd-b6789319ece0', 'word_count': 706, 'uri': 'nyt://article/66ac2cf0-f9f6-5b73-80fd-b6789319ece0'}, {'abstract': 'His journalism was unlike any I’d ever read, sympathetic and evocative and inventive, but also sharp-eyed and precise and acerbic.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/05/15/opinion/tom-wolfe-dead-new-journalism.html', 'snippet': 'His journalism was unlike any I’d ever read, sympathetic and evocative and inventive, but also sharp-eyed and precise and acerbic.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the summer after ninth grade, 1969, when I checked out a Tom Wolfe book from the Omaha Public Library, I had no real idea who he was, although he’d been a celebrity for a few years already. Not as famous as my favorite living writer at the time, Kurt Vonnegut, but famous enough that in Mr. Vonnegut’s rave review of Mr. Wolfe’s first book in this newspaper in 1965, he wrote, “Everybody talks about him.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/15/opinion/15andersen/merlin_138205959_637626f9-5ff5-4348-ad93-e65bf0a728e7-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 887, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/05/15/opinion/15andersen/merlin_138205959_637626f9-5ff5-4348-ad93-e65bf0a728e7-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 887}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/15/opinion/15andersen/merlin_138205959_637626f9-5ff5-4348-ad93-e65bf0a728e7-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 693, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'sub