89c6-58f6-b706-ecd0b6b0086f', 'word_count': 1007, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f7044ec1-89c6-58f6-b706-ecd0b6b0086f'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Simon and 16 musicians balanced grooves, hits, hope and darkness on the first night of “Homeward Bound — the Farewell Tour.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/05/17/arts/music/paul-simon-farewell-tour-review.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Simon and 16 musicians balanced grooves, hits, hope and darkness on the first night of “Homeward Bound — the Farewell Tour.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'VANCOUVER, British Columbia — Paul Simon couldn’t resist introducing some ambiguity to the first night of what he has billed as “Homeward Bound — the Farewell Tour,” at Rogers Arena here on Wednesday night. After singing “50 Ways to Leave Your Lover” — itself a song about an ambivalent exit — he joked: “I lied about the final. I was just trying to raise the ticket prices.” Then he backtracked. “Yeah. This is it.” But he continued, “I don’t know what the ‘it’ is,” and went on to elaborate. “Is this about the final iteration of these songs? Is this, like, where I’m freezing them? I honestly don’t know what the thing is.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/18/arts/18paulsimon-live1/18paulsimon-live1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/05/18/arts/18paulsimon-live1/18paulsimon-live1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/18/arts/18paulsimon-live1/18paulsimon-live1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/18/arts/18paulsimon-live1/18paulsimon-live1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/18/arts/18paulsimon-live1/18paulsimon-live1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/05/18/arts/18paulsimon-live1/18paulsimon-live1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/18/arts/18paulsimon-live1/18paulsimon-live1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: Paul Simon, Still Evolving, Is Saying Goodbye', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Still Evolving, but Saying Goodbye to the Road', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pop and Rock Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Simon, Paul', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-05-17T13:47:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Pareles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pareles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8dc9ec14-9674-5c81-96af-f47e93feff64', 'word_count': 916, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8dc9ec14-9674-5c81-96af-f47e93feff64'}, {'abstract': 'The guitarist from Niger is known for opening ears and minds with his virtuosic desert blues. For his new album, “Deran,” he returned to Africa.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/05/17/arts/music/bombino-desert-blues-deran.html', 'snippet': 'The guitarist from Niger is known for opening ears and minds with his virtuosic desert blues. For his new album, “Deran,” he returned to Africa.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On a recent night in Williamsburg, an ecstatic crowd jammed the dance floor of the club Brooklyn Bowl to see one of the world’s greatest living blues guitarists. He doesn’t hail from the Mississippi Delta or Chicago’s South Side, but from the dusty outpost of Agadez, Niger, in the Sahara. Oumara Moctar, better known as Bombino, is already a star among the Tuareg — the nomadic Berbers who traverse the countries along the desert — and as he releases his sixth album, “Deran,” he’s on the verge of becoming one internationally.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/20/arts/20bombino1/merlin_137989506_e2b39d20-345a-40e9-b04a-807225e29c2b-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 750, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/05/20/arts/20bombino1/merlin_137989506_e2b39d20-345a-40e9-b04a-807225e29c2b-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 750}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/20/arts/20bombino1/merlin_137989506_e2b39d20-345a-40e9-b04a-807225e29c2b-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 820, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/20/arts/20bombino1/merlin_137989506_e2b39d20-345a-40e9-b04a-807225e29c2b-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1639, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/20/arts/20bombino1/20bombino1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width