nd of youth, in Curtis Sittenfeld’s midlife-themed story collection “You Think It, I’ll Say It.” The end of an era (the era of sea battles), in Jan Morris’s contemplative history “Battleship Yamato.” The end of democracies, in Timothy Snyder’s sobering new analysis, “The Road to Unfreedom.” And lest you turn for comfort to spring — that traditional metaphor for rebirth and renewal — I have bleak news for you: Karl Ove Knausgaard’s “Spring,” the latest entry in his seasonal cycle, is haunted by the implied end of a marriage. The consolation, though, is that all of these books are excellent, as bracing and astringent as a slap. In writing as in menu planning, sometimes the ending is the most delicious part.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/20/books/review/20ed-covers/20ed-covers-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 301, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/05/20/books/review/20ed-covers/20ed-covers-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 301}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/20/books/review/20ed-covers/20ed-covers-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 505, 'width': 1005, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/20/books/review/20ed-covers/20ed-covers-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 505, 'width': 1005, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/20/books/review/20ed-covers/20ed-covers-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/05/20/books/rev