-54d6-a11f-3fa29779526d', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://audio/7fb2b56c-829f-54d6-a11f-3fa29779526d'}, {'abstract': 'A drone drops out of the sky, spiraling down like a bird that has been shot. Kids run to where it hits the ground: Yes, Israelis also lose things.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/05/17/opinion/in-gaza-may-is-the-cruelest-month.html', 'snippet': 'A drone drops out of the sky, spiraling down like a bird that has been shot. Kids run to where it hits the ground: Yes, Israelis also lose things.', 'lead_paragraph': 'JABALIYA CAMP, Gaza Strip — For the past seven weeks, every Friday morning a small group of teenagers had met at the corner of my street. They would exchange “Good mornings,” chat amicably for a while and then head east to the protests at the border with Israel. On Monday, they gathered again, this time to demonstrate against the opening of the new American embassy in Jerusalem. Tuesday was supposed to be the protests’ climax: It commemorated Nakba, the catastrophe, when Palestinians were expelled from our land by Israel in 1948. But somehow President Trump managed to add a day to our calendar of infamy, and it may be the crassest yet.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/17/opinion/17abusaifNAKBA/17abusaifNAKBA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 411, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/05/17/opinion/17abusaifNAKBA/17abusaifNAKBA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 411}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/17/opinion/17abusaifNAKBA/17abusaifNAKBA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 701, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/17/opinion/17abusaifNAKBA