e': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/03a016c8-6e86-5382-b8db-fd86d21a0767', 'word_count': 822, 'uri': 'nyt://article/03a016c8-6e86-5382-b8db-fd86d21a0767'}, {'abstract': 'Some regulation helps markets and the economy, some hurts. Guess which type is being slashed?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/05/17/opinion/trump-deregulation-republicans.html', 'snippet': 'Some regulation helps markets and the economy, some hurts. Guess which type is being slashed?', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Republicans look at what they’ve gotten out of their current moment of unified government, they can point to cutting corporate taxes, some judicial appointments and … not much else. Beyond that, they claim that they’ve teed up the economy for explosive growth through the magic of “deregulation.” But deregulation is a term that should be banned from the nation’s policy lexicon, mixing as it does equal parts wholesome and foul — in this administration, almost exclusively foul.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/17/opinion/17LindseyTeles/merlin_138293952_ca9432fd-1169-4df0-b3ae-4c1001982153-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/05/17/opinion/17LindseyTeles/merlin_138293952_ca9432fd-1169-4df0-b3ae-4c1001982153-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/17/opinion/17LindseyTeles/merlin_138293952_ca9432fd-1169-4df0-b3ae-4c1001982153-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/17/opinion/17LindseyTel