name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rashad, Phylicia', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-05-21T01:45:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jesse Green', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jesse', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Green', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/5d9be7b5-53a6-594f-92e5-f85b2d2f7e81', 'word_count': 1008, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5d9be7b5-53a6-594f-92e5-f85b2d2f7e81'}, {'abstract': 'Just when the dots were starting to connect, Season 2 introduced a whole new batch of mysteries. Here’s what we hope gets answered.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/05/20/watching/westworld-season-2-midseason-questions.html', 'snippet': 'Just when the dots were starting to connect, Season 2 introduced a whole new batch of mysteries. Here’s what we hope gets answered.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On a show in which identities, timelines and memories are perpetually scrambled or manipulated, there are bound to be questions, usually starting with, “What did I just watch?” But the first season of HBO’s “Westworld” was careful to dot the i’s and cross the t’s on many of the big questions it raised. We discovered the secret that the Man in Black was seeking at the center of the The Maze. We confirmed the popular theory that he and William were the same person on separate timelines. And we finally learned the true identity of “Wyatt,” and the true nature of Bernard.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/20/watching/20westworld-questions-8/20westworld-questions-8-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 337, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/05/20/watching/20westworld-questions-8/20westworld-questions-8-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 337}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/20/watching/20westworld-questions-8/20westworld-questions-8-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/20/watching/20westworld-questions-8/20westworld-questions-8-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1152, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/20/watching/20westworld-questions-8/20westworld-questions-8-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/05/20/watching/20westworld-questions-8/20westworld-questions-8-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/20/watching/20westworld-questions-8/20westworld-questions-8-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Westworld’ Season 2: Seven Big Things We Still Don’t Know at Midseason', 'kicker': 'Westworld', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Westworld (TV Program)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-05-21T02:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Watching', 'section_name': 'Watching', 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott Tobias', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tobias', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/423a3311-b5e8-5ca1-9558-cd10f280f645', 'word_count': 1397, 'uri': 'nyt://article/423a3311-b5e8-5ca1-9558-cd10f280f645'}, {'abstract': 'Their focus off each other, Bobby and Chuck enter into new high-stakes rivalries — this time with fish significantly bigger than themselves.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/05/20/arts/television/billions-recap-icebreaker.html', 'snippet': 'Their focus off each other, Bobby and Chuck enter into new high-stakes rivalries — this time with fish significantly bigger than themselves.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You can take the boys out of the blood feud, but you can’t take the blood feud out of the boys. Just two episodes after the successful conclusion of the truce that saw the main men of “Billions” call an end to hostilities and help each other out of potentially career-ending legal trouble, both Bobby Axelrod and Chuck Rhoades have launched dangerous new contests of the will. And this time around, it’s not the courtroom versus the boardroom: Each man has entered into a rivalry with a bigger fish in their own professional pond.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/18/arts/television/20billions-recap/20westworld-recap-slide-AD1F-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/05/18/arts/television/20billions-recap/20westworld-recap-slide-AD1F-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 450}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/18/arts/television/20billions-recap/20westworld-recap-slide-AD1F-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/18/arts/television/20billions-recap/20westworld-recap-slide-AD1F-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1536, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/18/arts/television/20billions-recap/20billions-recap-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/05/18/arts/television/20billions-recap/20billions-recap-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/18/arts/television/20billions-recap/20billions-recap-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Billions’ Season 3, Episode 9 Recap: Bear Market', 'kicker': 'Billions', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Billions (TV Program)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-05-21T02:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sean T. Collins', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sean', 'middlename': 'T.', 'lastname': 'Collins', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/5a313ec9-9715-53d7-9634-9ff3efe3de44', 'word_count': 1082, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5a313ec9-9715-53d7-9634-9ff3efe3de44'}, {'abstract': '“Westworld” expanded into Shogun World this week, and the self-aware hosts must ask themselves: What parts of their programming do they want to keep?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/05/20/arts/television/westworld-recap-akane-no-mai.html', 'snippet': '“Westworld” expanded into Shogun World this week, and the self-aware hosts must ask themselves: What parts of their programming do they want to keep?', 'lead_paragraph': 'There’s the best kind of Easter egg, a Joni Mitchell Easter egg, tucked into a crucial piece of dialogue in this week’s episode of “Westworld,” as Dolores and Teddy are talking about their feelings for each other. “I’m not some stranger, Dolores, coming from outside, looking for a pretty lie,” says Teddy. “I’ve known you my whole life. Where we go, we go eyes open. Together.” The phrase “pretty lie” recalls “The Last Time I Saw Richard,” the closing track on Mitchell’s seminal 1971 album, “Blue.” Mitchell is in dialogue with a fellow romantic, picking through the mistakes of the past, when they’ve left themselves vulnerable to partners who ultimately disappointed them.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/18/arts/television/20westworld-recap/20westworld-recap-slide-KN3P-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/05/18/arts/television/20westworld-recap/20westworld-recap-slide-KN3P-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/18/arts/television/20westworld-recap/20westworld-recap-slide-KN3P-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/18/arts/television/20westworld-recap/20westworld-recap-slide-KN3P-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo