 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fa93c1ff-c7f6-5c6d-8e9e-639e4e721635', 'word_count': 124, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fa93c1ff-c7f6-5c6d-8e9e-639e4e721635'}, {'abstract': 'Pack these canned Spanish mussels, from the outdoor clothing company Patagonia, as an elegant nibble for camping and picnics.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/05/21/dining/patagonia-provisions-mussels.html', 'snippet': 'Pack these canned Spanish mussels, from the outdoor clothing company Patagonia, as an elegant nibble for camping and picnics.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For several years, Patagonia, the outdoor clothing and gear company, has been selling handy, shelf-stable foods, mainly meant for camping. But they are also worth having on hand for summer picnics, in the park or on the beach. The latest in the Patagonia Provisions line is mussels, plump little morsels neatly packed in tins and available in three flavors: lemon-herb, sofrito (peppers and onions) and smoked. All are bathed in olive oil and mussel broth. They’re from a small fishery in Galicia, in Spain, a country that probably does a better job than any other when it comes to canned seafood: Patagonia Provisions Mussels, $8 for 4.2 ounces, three tins for $23, patagoniaprovisions.com.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/23/dining/23burnermussels/23burnermussels-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 520, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/05/23/dining/23burnermussels/23burnermussels-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 520}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo