tle': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/437721b8-3bf4-5b02-b24e-2524730dd30c', 'word_count': 899, 'uri': 'nyt://article/437721b8-3bf4-5b02-b24e-2524730dd30c'}, {'abstract': 'Hunan noodles in the East Village, additions to Gotham Market at the Ashland, an East End roundup and other restaurant news.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/05/22/dining/bistro-pierre-lapin-nyc-restaurant-news.html', 'snippet': 'Hunan noodles in the East Village, additions to Gotham Market at the Ashland, an East End roundup and other restaurant news.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BISTRO PIERRE LAPIN Though the chef Harold Moore is best known for comfort food (previously at Commerce, now Harold’s Meat & Three), he had French training, and he’s showing it off at his new bistro. Tapping into a few trends, like the increasingly ubiquitous all-day cafe and the proliferation of French fare in Lower Manhattan, this vintage-style corner spot starts with breakfast and runs right through dinner. Julia Grossman, Mr. Moore’s partner in this venture, is the baker turning out croissants, brioches and such for breakfast and beyond, paired with Mr. Moore’s homey pâtés, terrines, mushroom fricassees and Parisian sandwich fillings. Classics like pike quenelles, roast chicken for two with foie gras stuffing, leg of lamb with potato gratin, and several seasonal vegetable preparations bolster the menu. Desserts, including coeur à la crème for two, are Ms. Grossman’s responsibility, as are the wine- and liqueur-based cocktails: 99 Bank Street (Hudson Street), 212-858-6600, pierresnyc.com.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/23/dining/23off/23off-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarg