ews_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/da1b576c-30f2-5190-abd5-3784fca8f5a6', 'word_count': 983, 'uri': 'nyt://article/da1b576c-30f2-5190-abd5-3784fca8f5a6'}, {'abstract': 'From pale weisswurst to versatile kielbasa and beyond, there’s a lot to like in those casings.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/05/22/dining/best-sausages-summer.html', 'snippet': 'From pale weisswurst to versatile kielbasa and beyond, there’s a lot to like in those casings.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It is one of the great American pleasures to stand by a gas pump in southern Louisiana in punishing heat and humidity, squeezing boudin into your mouth. The combination of fatty minced pork and mineral-bright liver and melting bell pepper and soft salty rice is otherworldly, and might make boudin the greatest sausage there is — except that boudin isn’t really a sausage as much as the product of a sausage-casing delivery system, a tube to discard once you’re done eating.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/23/dining/23sausage-alt/23sausage-alt-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 398, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/05/23/dining/23sausage-alt/23sausage-alt-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 398}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/23/dining/23sausage-alt/23sausage-alt-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 680, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', '