'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-05-24T19:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Erin Aubry Kaplan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Erin', 'middlename': 'Aubry', 'lastname': 'Kaplan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/d87f3930-5ab5-55b5-bfd2-4f464528b364', 'word_count': 1159, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d87f3930-5ab5-55b5-bfd2-4f464528b364'}, {'abstract': 'In Erin Entrada Kelly’s charming new book, “You Go First,” misfits connect long-distance via online Scrabble. Is it an escape, or the start of a real bond?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/05/24/books/review/erin-entrada-kelly-you-go-first.html', 'snippet': 'In Erin Entrada Kelly’s charming new book, “You Go First,” misfits connect long-distance via online Scrabble. Is it an escape, or the start of a real bond?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Scramble some of the letters in the word xylitol, and you can find the words toil, toll and till. And the words waiting room will yield migration. Such verbal gymnastics are on offer in Erin Entrada Kelly’s emotionally rich and charming novel YOU GO FIRST (Greenwillow, 288 pp., $16.99; ages 8 to 12) because words are of great interest to her two main characters, who meet in a school-sanctioned online Scrabble message board and form a friendship while playing the game itself. Words dance and bend and reorganize themselves to the will of 12-year-old Charlotte Lockard, who is particularly resourceful at moving letters around. And 11-year-old Ben Boxer, who reaches first place on the leaderboard, has a strong command over words as well. (Charlotte and Ben are respectively known online as Lottie Lock and Ben Boot.) If only the rest of their lives were as easily managed. But in fact both Charlotte and Ben find themselves in difficult situations, and no amount of clever rearranging can possibly help.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/27/books/review/27KIDS1/27KIDS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 894, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/05/27/books/review/27KIDS1/27KIDS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 894}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/27/books/review/27KIDS1/27KIDS1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 687, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/27/books/review/27KIDS1/27KIDS1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1346, 'width': 903, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/27/books/review/27KIDS1/27KIDS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/05/27/books/review/27KIDS1/27KIDS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/27/books/review/27KIDS1/27KIDS1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In a Newbery Medalist’s Latest Novel, Friends Find Solace in Words', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Standout Novels', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'You Go First (Book)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kelly, Erin Entrada', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2018-05-24T19:00:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Meg Wolitzer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Meg', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wolitzer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2aa2c851-596a-518c-b0d5-357d17c0ae56', 'word_count': 708, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2aa2c851-596a-518c-b0d5-357d17c0ae56'}, {'abstract': 'The singer and songwriter has spun tragedy into plainly angry action on her new album, “Hell-On.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/05/24/arts/music/neko-case-interview-hell-on.html', 'snippet': 'The singer and songwriter has spun tragedy into plainly angry action on her new album, “Hell-On.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'The fire itself — the one that decimated Neko Case’s Vermont home last year, reducing her refuge of more than a decade to what she called “a hole in the ground” — wasn’t even the worst part.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/25/arts/25nekocase1/merlin_137997966_5ff24a10-6c33-46b1-97c9-6f089a7dd122-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/05/25/arts/25nekocase1/merlin_137997966_5ff24a10-6c33-46b1-97c9-6f089a7dd122-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 450}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/25/arts/25nekocase1/merlin_137997966_5ff24a10-6c33-46b1-97c9-6f089a7dd122-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 