d982dbc-98ac-5653-be70-f8cd0b61c65d', 'word_count': 484, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3d982dbc-98ac-5653-be70-f8cd0b61c65d'}, {'abstract': 'The frequency of random violence has spread anxiety beyond its targets and into our everyday life.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2018/05/28/opinion/fear-mistrust-in-public-space.html', 'snippet': 'The frequency of random violence has spread anxiety beyond its targets and into our everyday life.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In March — sometime between the time 17 high school students and educators were massacred by a lone shooter in Parkland, Fla., and 10 more were killed in Santa Fe, Tex. — a school district in Pennsylvania furnished all of its 200 classrooms with weapons — in this case, buckets of rocks for students to throw at would-be school shooters. A few weeks later, another of the state’s school districts armed its teachers with miniature baseball bats. One can hardly blame school administrators for trying to do something — anything — to protect the children and themselves at a time in which school massacres have become commonplace enough to characterize as “a unique American ritual.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/28/opinion/28stone-1/merlin_136945665_66c4d9c5-3511-4d58-adef-4d648587dcf6-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 389, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/05/28/opinion/28stone-1/merlin_136945665_66c4d9c5-3511-4d58-adef-4d648587dcf6-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 389}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/05/28/opinion/28stone-1/merlin_136945665_66c4d9c5-3511-4d58-adef-4d648587dcf6-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 664, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': '